---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="2.3 Leadership and Management">
  <div class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/ibbm/">Home</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <a href="/ibbm/unit2/">Unit 2</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <span>2.3 Leadership and Management</span>
  </div>
  <div class="topic-hero">
    <span class="topic-number">2.3</span>
    <h1>Leadership and Management</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">What leaders and managers do, and which style fits which situation.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tabs">
    <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="switchTab(this,'notes')">Notes</button>
    <button class="tab-btn" onclick="switchTab(this,'practice')">Practice</button>
  </div>

  <!-- NOTES -->
  <div id="notes" class="tab-content active">

    <div class="learning-goals">
      <div class="learning-goals-title">Learning Goals</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Distinguish management from leadership</li>
        <li>Evaluate five leadership styles and when each works</li>
        <li>Explain scientific and intuitive thinking <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Management vs leadership</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Management</th><th>Leadership</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Directing a business's resources to achieve its objectives</td>
            <td>Using strategic and creative thinking to inspire people to meet challenges and reach goals</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Planning; coordinating; commanding and controlling; resolving problems</td>
            <td>Sets a vision and long-term goals; motivates; builds trust; challenges the status quo; resolves problems</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Is one better? Not always. Most businesses need both, often from the same person.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Five leadership styles</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Style</th><th>What the leader does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Autocratic</strong></td><td>Makes the decisions. Strong and rule-oriented</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Paternalistic</strong></td><td>Looks after employees like family and expects loyalty</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Democratic</strong></td><td>Seeks input and involves employees in decisions</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Laissez-faire</strong></td><td>Hands-off: lets the team make the decisions</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Situational</strong></td><td>Adapts the style to the situation</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="callout exam-tip">
        <div class="callout-title">Situational leadership</div>
        <p>The leader adapts to the place, the time, the people and the task. No single style is always best: a crisis may call for autocratic leadership; a skilled team may work best with laissez-faire or democratic. Useful when leading a large organization with diverse needs.</p>
      </div>
      <p><strong>Culture and ethics</strong> also shape which style works. A "know your place" society may accept autocratic leaders; a "live and let live" society may expect democratic or laissez-faire ones. Ethics can rule some choices out altogether.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Scientific and intuitive thinking <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></h2>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">HL only, but useful for SL too.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Scientific</th><th>Intuitive</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Decisions use</strong></td><td>Quantitative research and techniques</td><td>Experience and fast reasoning, not only data</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Benefits</strong></td><td>Efficient, accurate, less bias</td><td>Faster; suits emergencies</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Limits</strong></td><td>Faulty assumptions; data hard to get; slower</td><td>Not based on data; riskier</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Example</strong></td><td>Taylor's scientific management</td><td>Ford doubling wages in 1914</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-title">Recap</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Managers direct resources to objectives; leaders inspire people towards a vision</li>
        <li>Autocratic, paternalistic, democratic, laissez-faire, situational: each fits some situations</li>
        <li>Situational leaders switch style with the place, time, people and task</li>
        <li>HL: scientific thinking uses data; intuitive uses experience and speed</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div><!-- /notes -->

  <!-- PRACTICE -->
  <div id="practice" class="tab-content">

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 1: Leaders you respect</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Think of a teacher, coach or club leader you really like. What do they do? Now one you find hard to work with. What do they do? What's the difference?</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 2: What should be done about Alex?</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Alex joined your cafe team three months ago. She is polite and always on time, but orders are often wrong and tasks are left half done. Alex rarely asks for help and seems unsure what to do. Regular customers have started to complain, and other staff are covering for Alex and getting frustrated.</p>
        <div class="exercise-q">Write four different ideas for what should be done. Then sort them under the five leadership styles. Which style has the most? Which has none? For each style, note a strength, a risk, and a workplace where it would really work.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 3: Manager or leader?</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="sort-grid">
          <div class="sort-item">Plans next week's schedule<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Manager</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Develops a vision for the business in ten years<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Leader</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Solves a day-to-day problem<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Manager</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Inspires people to take on a challenge<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Leader</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Checks that tasks and projects are completed<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Manager</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Challenges the status quo<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Leader</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Makes sure everyone is doing their assigned duties<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Manager</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Builds trust in the organization<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Leader</span></button></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 4: Review questions</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Use these to guide your notes. Define each key term in your own words as you go.</div>
        <ol style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>What is scientific thinking and management? <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></li>
          <li>What is intuitive thinking and management? <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></li>
          <li>What is the main advantage of each? <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></li>
          <li>How does management differ from leadership?</li>
          <li>What are the main functions of management?</li>
          <li>For each of autocratic, paternalistic, democratic, laissez-faire and situational: what is it, and when might it suit?</li>
          <li>What five factors affect situational leadership? (Hint: the mnemonic "CLOTS")</li>
          <li>How do cultural differences and ethical considerations affect leadership style?</li>
          <li>Which leadership style would you expect to use intuitive thinking? Which scientific? <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 5: Exam practice</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Using a real world example, explain two differences between a leader and a manager. [4 marks]</div>
        <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--text-muted);">Seven minutes. Then mark it, or swap with a partner.</p>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">Marking guide</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">For each of the two differences: 1 mark for identifying a difference, 1 mark for applying it to an example.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 6: Spot the style</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Write one sentence for each style: a business or leader you know that shows it.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div><!-- /practice -->

  <div class="page-nav">
    <a href="/ibbm/unit2/2-2/" class="prev">
      <span class="nav-label">← Previous</span>
      <span class="nav-title">2.2 Organizational Structure</span>
    </a>
    <a href="/ibbm/unit2/" class="next">
      <span class="nav-label">Next →</span>
      <span class="nav-title">Unit 2</span>
    </a>
  </div>
</Layout>
